{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/5508997-0": {
      "title": "Part time faculty for Math and Science Department (71103)",
      "description": "Part time faculty for Math and Science Department (71103) Salary $2,050.00 - $2,350.00 Hourly Location Brooklyn, NY, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202600411 Division School of Liberal Arts & Sciences Department Math and Science Opening Date 10/07/2026 Closing Date 11/7/2026 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description The Department of Mathematics and Science in the School of Liberal Arts and Sciences at Pratt Institute (Brooklyn and Manhattan Campuses) invites applications as part of an open call to join our pool of part-time faculty. The department provides foundational and advanced STEM coursework tailored for emerging artists, designers, and architects. We are seeking qualified part-time instructors to teach undergraduate lecture and laboratory/studio-integrated courses across the following curricular areas: Mathematics & Quantitative Reasoning Computational & Applied Sciences, including coding and AI literacy Physical Sciences Chemical & Materials Sciences Earth, Environmental, & Ecological Sciences Life & Biological Sciences Our courses contribute to Pratt’s General Education program in support of our diverse creative majors. Following a studio schedule (one three-hour meeting per week), we maintain small class sizes (ranging from 15 to 22 students). Devoted to a creative learning community, Pratt recognizes the strength that stems from diverse perspectives, ideas, backgrounds, approaches, and experiences. We aspire to continue to grow as a place that always welcomes and encourages individuals of all backgrounds to contribute to and be part of Pratt culture; this aspiration is supported by Pratt’s Office of Engagement, Culture and Community. With a robust Center for Teaching and Learning focused on inclusive teaching and trauma-informed pedagogy, Pratt allows faculty to develop the approaches needed to meet the diverse needs of our students. Qualifications A graduate degree in a relevant STEM field is highly preferred, but strong candidates who are progressing towards a graduate degree are also encouraged to apply. We value teaching experience from both traditional and non-traditional instructional settings. Supplemental Information Review of applications will begin immediately; this position is an open call, so the review will be continuous. To apply, please submit your C.V. and cover letter outlining your disciplinary expertise, relevant teaching philosophy, and specific courses or subject areas you are qualified to instruct. Professional references will be requested later in the interview process. Candidates should be prepared to provide at least three professional references, including two current or former managers/supervisors. We encourage submitting up to five references total to ensure alternate contacts are available if needed. Pay Rate: $2,050 – $2,350 per contact hour with standard 3 credits per course (or prevailing rate in accordance with the collective bargaining agreement). This is a Local 1460 UFCT position. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. Commuter Benefits (Transit & Parking) Disability Benefits Voluntary Short Term Disability Voluntary Supplemental Retirement Plan Paid Time Off (Sick) Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/5501882-0": {
      "title": "Visiting Assistant Professor - School of Information (71103)",
      "description": "Visiting Assistant Professor - School of Information (71103) Salary $1,800.00 - $2,000.00 Hourly Location New York, NY, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202600413 Division School of Information Department School of Information Opening Date 10/01/2026 Closing Date 10/19/2026 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description Pratt Institute School of Information is a graduate school based in Manhattan enrolling more than 400 students across master’s programs in Library & Information Science, Information Experience Design, Data Analytics & Visualization, and Museums & Digital Culture. The School of Information seeks a Visiting Assistant Professor to teach the following course, offered January 25, 2027 to May 14, 2027, in the evenings, in-person at the Pratt Manhattan Center at 144 W. 14th Street: INFO625: Archival Theory and Practice This foundational course introduces students to core archival functions, including appraisal, acquisition, arrangement, description, reference, access, and outreach. Students will explore fundamental archival concepts, terminology, values, and ethics while examining diverse types of repositories. The course traces the history and evolution of the archival profession and addresses current debates shaping archival practice, with a particular focus on archival arrangement and description. Emphasizing hands-on learning, students will arrange and describe a small collection, working with Describing Archives: A Content Standard (DACS), and creating a finding aid in ArchivesSpace. The course also highlights how societal values, cultural understanding, and technological advances influence contemporary approaches to organizing and describing archival materials, including efforts to address historical inequities and partner with diverse communities, preparing students with the integrated theoretical knowledge and practical skills necessary for professional archival practice. INFO635: Archival Appraisal Advanced seminar on archival appraisal and acquisition theory and practice, examining how collecting decisions shape collective memory and social documentation in analog and digital contexts. Emphasizes institutional and community influences, power dynamics, and strategies for more inclusive and equitable collecting through critique of historical appraisal biases. INFO646: Digital Product Design This course focuses on the process, practices, and tools for designing engaging, understandable, and technically feasible digital products. Students will learn about and apply advanced techniques and tools relevant to the entire product design lifecycle, including identifying, investigating, and validating design problems, as well as crafting, designing, and testing digital solutions. Students will also learn how to visualize and effectively communicate design insights to various stakeholders. INFO672: UX Design Systems A user experience (UX) design system is a version-controlled collection of reusable user interface patterns and a set of associated guidelines, principles, and standards. A UX design system allows design teams to efficiently create consistent experiences across a range of digital products. Combining concepts from systems thinking, pattern languages, and atomic design, in this course students will learn how to analyze, use, and create a UX design system. Topics will also include coding and designing for accessibility, creating and using brand and voice guidelines, and design system governance policies and processes. INFO676: Early Childhood The course explores the specialized skills and knowledge needed to design and develop library programs, services, and resources for infants and young children (0-8 years) The course will follow the literacy continuum, from language development through to independent reading writing, with a special focus on the first three years of life, early literacy, and the role of family and community. This course will interest students who want to work with young people in different contexts and need specialized knowledge of early childhood, the social dynamic of families, and the early education environment. Qualifications The candidate should have a master’s degree in a relevant field and have an aptitude for teaching. For questions about the course, please email Assistant Dean, Jonathan Williams at jwill100@pratt.edu . Supplemental Information The School of Information at Pratt Institute is a graduate school based in Manhattan enrolling 350 students across masters programs in Library & Information Science, Information Experience Design, Data Analytics & Visualization, and Museums and Digital Culture. All of our courses are offered in-person in Manhattan at 144 W. 14 th St. Total compensation per class is $7,000 - $8,000. This is a UFCT Local 1460 union position. Deadline for application is Monday, October 19, 2026 ***To apply, please upload a copy of your CV and a cover letter. If moved forward, professional references will be requested. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. Commuter Benefits (Transit & Parking) Disability Benefits Voluntary Short Term Disability Voluntary Supplemental Retirement Plan Paid Time Off (Sick) Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/5469143-0": {
      "title": "STEAM Lab Instructor (71114)",
      "description": "STEAM Lab Instructor (71114) Salary $90.00 Hourly Location Brooklyn, NY, NY Job Type Non-Credit Continuing Ed Lecturers Job Number 202600394 Division Provost - Foundation & K-12 Department K-12 Center Opening Date 09/01/2026 Closing Date 6/27/2027 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description Pratt STEAM Lab provides free in-school and after-school classes to over 260 middle school and high school students annually. We offer rigorous science, technology, engineering, art, and mathematics (STEAM) programming, augmented by academic tutoring, SAT prep and college counseling. Our program draws on students’ innate curiosity, imagination and creativity to introduce them to a wide range of scientific and technological fields. Classes are run at partner schools, at Pratt’s state-of-the-art maker space at the Brooklyn Navy Yard, and on Pratt’s main campus. Pratt STEAM Lab is funded by New York State STEP (Science & Technology Entry Program), which aims to increase the number of economically disadvantaged students prepared to enter college, and improve their participation rate in mathematics, science, technology, health-related fields, and the licensed professions. Pratt STEAM Lab seeks exceptional instructors for classes and workshops that vary in length from one day workshops to 10-week courses meeting once a week. Pratt STEAM Lab seeks to maintain a robust pool of instructors by accepting applications from new instructors to teach established non-credit courses and workshops to students ages 12 to 18. Current offerings include Animation, Creative Computing, Sustainable Fashion, Bio-mimicry, Woodworking, Plant Based Dyes and Scientific Illustration, Weaving and Industrial Design. Additional areas of interest include Bio-Materials, 3d Printing. Urban Design and Net Zero Architecture. Instructors are expected to develop their own syllabi and to ensure that their classes fit cohesively into the larger program structure. Examples of Duties Develop and teach non-credit courses or workshops to middle school and/or high school aged students in science, technology, engineering, math, and design, that incorporate art. Create syllabi and material lists. Propose new courses based on area of expertise and engage with students through in-person classes. Qualifications Education: BA, BS, BFA, MFA, MS or MA or equivalent required in relevant art and design and/or STEM field. Experience: REQUIRED: Candidates must have expertise in their field Minimum 1 year experience as a teaching artist, teaching in-person classes. Must be available to work afternoons when program is in session. Commitment to positive youth development and asset-based frameworks is required. PREFFERED: Previous teaching experience in a K-12 environment, including work with schools, community programs, museums or non-profits. Experience developing STEAM curricula. Available to travel to off-campus locations, as needed. Teaching experience with low-income youth in NYC is a plus. Skills: TEACHING Must have the ability to work successfully with middle school and high school aged youth, including the ability to establish a working classroom environment. Ability to develop a multi-session sequence of projects Ability to break projects into subtasks with specific skill development Must have the ability to communicate effectively with a diverse population of students and staff, as well as demonstrate an understanding of the dynamics of working in a diverse community education setting. INITIATIVE Must have demonstrated ability to work independently, think ahead, and take initiative. Creative and flexible problem-solver. Able to adapt to new challenges Commitment to ongoing growth and refinement of skills, open to feedback. Supplemental Information Applicants must complete an online application, including resume and cover letter through Pratt's website. Any application without a cover letter will not be considered. Professional references will be requested later in the interview process. Candidates should be prepared to provide at least three professional references, including two current or former managers/supervisors. Candidates will be evaluated based on their professional background, education and prior teaching experience. Ideal applicants will have experience teaching in-person courses. Ideal start date: September 2026 Salary: $90 per teaching hour Important Information About This Posting This is an evergreen posting designed to create a pool of qualified candidates for current and future opportunities. Applications are reviewed as hiring needs arise, and there may be extended periods between application submission and candidate outreach. If your qualifications align with a current or future opening, a member of our team may contact you to discuss next steps. Some positions may require candidates to be available for an immediate start. We appreciate your interest and thank you for your patience throughout the process. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/5446376-0": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty – Undergraduate Communications Design (71103)",
      "description": "Part-Time Faculty – Undergraduate Communications Design (71103) Salary $1,800.00 - $2,200.00 Hourly Location Brooklyn, NY, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202500284 Division School of Design Department UG Communications Design Opening Date 08/12/2026 Closing Date 12/31/2026 11:59 PM Eastern Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description The Undergraduate (UG) Communications Design (ComD) department at Pratt Institute is calling for applications for inclusion in the pool of candidates to join our part-time teaching faculty, starting from the Spring 2026 semester. The UG Communications Design department is committed to educate students to become creative and responsible design leaders capable of bringing positive changes in our discipline, our communities, society, and the environment. Whether working with industry or acting as entrepreneurs, ComD graduates are able to create dynamic art, communications, systems, and products which help inform and inspire others — recognizing the strength that stems from a diversity of perspectives, ideas, backgrounds, approaches and experiences. We seek applicants with a broad vision of the future of the design field and the ability to articulate that vision. We encourage applicants with experience and knowledge in the following areas of expertise: Dynamic imagery (motion graphics, digital animation, AfterEffects, etc.) Digital 3-d prototyping (Blender, Cinema 4D, etc.) Examples of Duties Teach part-time courses in the UG Communications Design department, focusing on areas such as creating dynamic imagery and/or 3D prototyping Teaching to both concentrations in these courses: Graphic Design and Illustration Contribute to an inclusive and collaborative learning environment by leveraging strong interpersonal skills and intercultural competence, engaging with diverse student populations to promote responsible design leadership. Qualifications Applicants are required to have a professional degree in communication design or relevant fields, and college-level teaching experience. Candidates must have excellent interpersonal skills, a demonstrated level of intercultural competence, and ability to work well with diverse populations. Supplemental Information Supplemental Information Review of applications will begin immediately and be ongoing. To apply, please submit your c.v., cover letter and contact information for two professional references. In addition, it is preferred that you provide a link to your professional portfolio which should demonstrate those skills, and student project samples. ** Please Note: We are currently unable to support new H-1b visas for candidates residing outside of the United States.** Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. Commuter Benefits (Transit & Parking) Disability Benefits Voluntary Short Term Disability Voluntary Supplemental Retirement Plan Paid Time Off (Sick) Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/4685372-0": {
      "title": "Continuing Education Instructors (71114)",
      "description": "Continuing Education Instructors (71114) Salary $60.00 - $120.00 Hourly Location Brooklyn, NY, NY Job Type Non-Credit Continuing Ed Lecturers Job Number 202400077 Division Continuing & Prof Studies Opening Date 03/16/2026 Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description Position Description The School of Continuing and Professional Studies at Pratt Institute (SCPS) is creating a pool of applicants for part-time, non-credit instructor appointments for upcoming semesters. SCPS offers a broad array of professional development non-credit courses, workshops and certificate programs in areas art, design and architecture. Our full catalogue of current course offerings can be found at pratt.edu/scps . Classes and workshops vary in length from one day workshops to full semester courses. The SCPS program seeks to maintain a robust pool of instructors by accepting applications from new instructors to teach established non-credit courses and workshops. Proposals for new courses centered around the instructor's area of expertise are also welcome. Non-credit instructors are expected to develop their own syllabi and participate in curriculum development meetings to ensure that their classes fit cohesively into the larger program structure. Devoted to a creative learning community, Pratt recognizes the strength that stems from a diversity of perspectives, ideas, backgrounds, approaches, and experiences. We aspire to continue to grow as a place that always welcomes and encourages individuals of all backgrounds to contribute to and be part of the Pratt culture. Examples of Duties Develop and teach non-credit courses or workshops in art, design, and architecture, including creating syllabi and participating in curriculum development meetings. Propose new courses based on area of expertise and engage with students through in-person, hybrid, and online learning platforms such as Canvas and Zoom. Qualifications Candidates must have expertise in their field and will be evaluated based on their professional background, education and prior teaching experience. Ideal applicants will have experience teaching in-person, hybrid and online courses. Experience with Canvas, Zoom and other online learning tools is preferred. Compensation and class dates depend on the nature and duration of the course or workshop. Application to this posting is a requirement for all new SCPS non-credit instructors. Pay Rate: $60 - $120/hour depending on course Supplemental Information Please submit your c.v. and cover letter. Please indicate in your cover letter if you are applying to teach an existing course or workshop or if you are proposing (pitching) a new one. **Professional references will be requested later in the interview process. Candidates should be prepared to provide at least three professional references, including two current or former managers/supervisors. We encourage submitting up to five references total to ensure alternate contacts are available if needed. ** Please Note: We are currently unable to support new H-1b visas for candidates residing outside of the United States.** Important Information About This Posting This is an evergreen posting designed to create a pool of qualified candidates for current and future opportunities. Applications are reviewed as hiring needs arise, and there may be extended periods between application submission and candidate outreach. If your qualifications align with a current or future opening, a member of our team may contact you to discuss next steps. Some positions may require candidates to be available for an immediate start. We appreciate your interest and thank you for your patience throughout the process. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. This position is not eligible for benefits. Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/4687251-0": {
      "title": "Part-Time Faculty - History of Art and Design (71103) EG",
      "description": "Part-Time Faculty - History of Art and Design (71103) EG Salary See Position Description Location Brooklyn, NY, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202400079 Division School of Liberal Arts & Sciences Department History of Art and Design Opening Date 01/07/2026 Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description The Department of the History of Art and Design at Pratt Institute (both Brooklyn and Manhattan Campuses), is creating a pool of part-time faculty applicants for the Fall 2026 semester to teach in the following areas: Art History Survey Courses (Ancient to Medieval; Renaissance to Modern/Contemporary) History of Design Survey Courses (History of Interior Design, History of Industrial Design, History of Communications Design) Asian Art African Art Latin American/Pre-Columbian Art New Media Devoted to a creative learning community, Pratt recognizes the strength that stems from a diversity of perspectives, ideas, backgrounds, approaches, and experiences. We aspire to continue to grow as a place that always welcomes and encourages individuals of all backgrounds to contribute to and be part of Pratt culture. Examples of Duties Teach part-time courses in Art History or Design History, covering areas such as Ancient to Medieval, Renaissance to Modern/Contemporary Art, or the History of Interior, Industrial, and Communications Design at both the Brooklyn and Manhattan campuses. Engage with a diverse student body by fostering an inclusive learning environment, leveraging expertise in specialized areas such as Asian Art, African Art, Latin American/Pre-Columbian Art, or New Media, while promoting the values of Pratt’s creative learning community. Qualifications Art History Survey Courses: we require that candidates have an MA in the History of Art or the History of Design; ABD or PhD, and teaching experience beyond the TA level is preferred. History of Design Survey Courses : we require that candidates have an MA in the History of Design or the History of Art; ABD or PhD, and teaching experience beyond the TA level is preferred. Asian Art, African Art, Latin American/Pre-Columbian Art, New Media: we require that candidates be at least ABD, PhD and teaching experience are preferred. Pay Rate: $1,800 - $1,950 per contact hour Supplemental Information Review of applications to begin immediately. To apply, please submit your c.v. and cover letter. **Professional references will be requested later in the interview process. Candidates should be prepared to provide at least three professional references, including two current or former managers/supervisors. We encourage submitting up to five references total to ensure alternate contacts are available if needed. ** Please Note: We are currently unable to support new H-1b visas for candidates residing outside of the United States.** Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. Commuter Benefits (Transit & Parking) Disability Benefits Voluntary Short Term Disability Voluntary Supplemental Retirement Plan Paid Time Off (Sick) Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    },
    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/pratt/jobs/4687153-0": {
      "title": "Visiting Faculty – Fashion Design Department (71103) EG",
      "description": "Visiting Faculty – Fashion Design Department (71103) EG Salary See Position Description Location Brooklyn, NY, NY Job Type Part-Time Faculty Job Number 202400075 Division School of Design Department Fashion Design Opening Date 10/08/2024 Description Benefits About Pratt Pratt Institute provides the creative leaders of tomorrow the professional knowledge and experience to make a better world. A world-class and internationally ranked college with programs in art, design, architecture, liberal arts and sciences, and information studies, Pratt offers nearly 50 undergraduate and graduate degree programs. The Institute’s impact expands beyond its 25-acre residential campus in Brooklyn to cutting-edge facilities throughout the borough, a landmark building and public gallery in Manhattan, as well as an extension campus, Pratt Munson College of Art and Design in Utica, New York. On Governors Island, Pratt is a core partner on the Stony Brook University team selected to develop the New York Climate Exchange, a research and educational hub poised to establish New York City as the global leader for the most pressing issue of our time: climate change. Another extension of Pratt’s campus is the Research Yard, a 20,000-square-foot facility that brings together the research activities of Pratt Institute under one roof in the Brooklyn Navy Yard (BNY). Since our founding in 1887, Pratt has upheld the belief that education should be accessible to all who wish to learn. As a community, we define ourselves as standard setters who are: Resourceful and collaborative problem solvers, Socially responsible and equity-centered creatives, Adaptable and resilient global stewards, Inquisitive and compassionate community members. Description The Fashion Design department at Pratt Institute, located in Brooklyn, NY, is creating a pool of applicants to join our part-time faculty community. At Pratt, we define fashion practice as cultural messaging through clothes. Students contextualize fashion within a social framework and through rigorous attention to production, craft, and contemporary aesthetics. Devoted to a creative learning community, Pratt recognizes the strength that stems from a diversity of perspectives, ideas, backgrounds, approaches and experiences. We aspire to continue to grow as a place that always welcomes and encourages individuals of all backgrounds to contribute to and be part of the Pratt community. Examples of Duties Teach part-time courses in the Fashion Design department, focusing on fashion as cultural messaging through production, craft, and contemporary aesthetics, while fostering an inclusive and diverse learning environment. Leverage expertise in areas such as sustainability, non-western fashion perspectives, digital storytelling, or fashion technology, to engage students in critical and innovative approaches to fashion design. Qualifications Candidate’s professional or scholarly focus may include a broad set of interests within the field including design, textiles, and knitwear. We encourage applicants with experience and knowledge in the following areas of expertise: Historical and critical perspectives on contemporary fashion design Non-western perspectives on fashion design Sustainability, materials and circular design practices Digital storytelling, networked design, augmented/virtual reality and CLO3D Technology, culture and fashion media Salary is competitive and commensurate with experience and qualifications, ranging from $1800/per contact hour. Supplemental Information Review of applications to begin immediately and remain open until filled. To apply, please submit your c.v., cover letter, and 10 examples of syllabi and/or student work and/or professional projects that reflect your pedagogical and professional practice. **Professional references will be requested later in the interview process. Candidates should be prepared to provide at least three professional references, including two current or former managers/supervisors. We encourage submitting up to five references total to ensure alternate contacts are available if needed. Please Note: We are unable to support new H-1B visas for candidates residing outside of the United States. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement We are an equal opportunity employer and do not discriminate in hiring or employment on the basis of race, color, creed, religion or belief, national or ethnic origin, citizenship status, marital or domestic partnership status, sexual orientation, sex, gender identity or expression, age, disability, military or veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law. Pratt Institute recognizes and values the benefits of a diverse workforce. At Pratt, we prioritize equity and have established a systematic approach to guarantee fair treatment for everyone. If your background and expertise align with the requirements of the position, you will be contacted for further instructions. Commuter Benefits (Transit & Parking) Disability Benefits Voluntary Short Term Disability Voluntary Supplemental Retirement Plan Paid Time Off (Sick) Employer Pratt Institute Address 200 Willoughby Ave Brooklyn, New York, 11205 Phone 718-636-3787 Website pratt.edu Apply"
    }
  },
